Inteligeo, load test of 2026-11-13 (no advisory, no upstream fix): a request to the
resource service API (delete, update, copy, permissions, create, ingest), and the
GeoServer cleanup that follows a deletion, are no longer dropped when they wait more
than 30 s for a Celery worker.

Target: GeoNode 4.3.1 as installed by this image (site-packages/geonode). Applied
with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` from site-packages (paths are geonode/...).

What happened: the six views of base/api/views.py that hand work to
resouce_service_dispatcher create an ExecutionRequest in `ready` and enqueue the task
with `expiration=30`, and the task itself is declared with `expires=30`. Celery 5.6
turns either into the AMQP per-message TTL (an explicit `expiration` on the call wins
over the task's `expires`): RabbitMQ discards a message that sits 30 s in the queue,
with no log line on either side and nothing left in the queue. The worker never sees
it, so the execution stays `ready` for good and the resource is neither deleted nor
changed. On the mirror, deleting the resources of a round through the API (about 230
DELETE /api/v2/resources/<pk>/delete in a few seconds) left 4 of 183, 44 of 231 and
43 of 232 executions in `ready`; a second DELETE of the same resources, sent when the
queue was short, deleted them all. The worker prefetches 60 messages, and with 10 to
15 processes the rest waited in RabbitMQ longer than 30 s. django_celery_results has
no REVOKED row for them, which the worker would have written had the message reached
it and expired there.

The deletion that does run then enqueues geoserver_cascading_delete (a dataset) or
geoserver_delete_map (a map) on the queue `cleanup`, with the same `expires=30` on
the task and `expiration=30` on the calls (geoserver/manager.py, and the two in
geoserver/signals.py). After a burst of deletions those can be dropped the same way,
leaving the layer, its store entry and styles in GeoServer, and the GeoFence rules,
which match by name, for a future layer of the same name. No dropped one was counted
in the load test (nothing in the logs tells), but the TTL is the same.

The change: the three tasks lose `expires=30` and the nine calls lose
`expiration=30`. A request waits in the queue until a worker takes it.

inteligeo-geonode copies its own base/api/views.py over GeoNode's (its Dockerfile),
so the six views served are that copy's: inteligeo-geonode drops `expiration=30` from
its six calls in the same change, and the two ship together. The hunks below for
base/api/views.py keep GeoNode's own file consistent in case the copy goes away.

What changes in behaviour:
- A request sent while the worker is down or busy runs when it gets to the worker,
  instead of vanishing after 30 s. The dispatcher still runs an execution only while
  it is `ready` and marks it `running` before starting.
- A user who repeats a request because the first seemed stuck now gets both run; the
  second delete of the same resource fails and is marked `failed`, as it already did
  when both arrived in time.
- There is no ordering between requests on the same resource (two edits may run in
  either order), as before within the 30 s; now it also holds for a request that
  waited longer.

Not changed: the other GeoServer tasks of geoserver/tasks.py (`expires=30`) and the
calls with `expiration=30` to them in geoserver/signals.py, and those in
notifications_helper.py, base/views.py, services/, br/ and harvesting; they share
the 30 s TTL but are not on the deletion path.
inteligeo-geonode's inteligeo5/geoserver/manager.py also has the two calls, but its
Dockerfile does not copy that file into GeoNode.

Not run: GeoNode's tests and a request against a running GeoNode. Checked by reading
Celery 5.6.3 (app/base.py send_task: `expires` becomes `options["expiration"]`, and an
`expiration` option passes through to the producer) and kombu 5.6.2 (Producer.publish:
`expiration` becomes the message property in ms).

